[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Headless CLI Execution and CI/CD Integration in JMeter"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Headless Execution"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Running Apache JMeter in a Graphical User Interface (GUI) mode consumes excessive system resources and is strictly recommended only for test creation and debugging. For performance testing, load generation, and CI/CD pipeline integration, JMeter must be executed in non-GUI (headless) mode via the Command Line Interface (CLI)."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"JMeter CLI Command Syntax"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"The standard command to execute a JMeter test plan from the terminal involves specifying the non-GUI flag, the input test plan file, and the output results file."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"jmeter -n -t path/to/test_plan.jmx -l path/to/results.jtl -e -o path/to/dashboard_folder"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"-n: Specifies JMeter should run in non-GUI mode."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"-t: Path to the .jmx test plan file."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"-l: Path to the results .jtl file generated during the test."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"-e: Generate HTML report dashboard upon test completion."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"-o: Path to the output directory for the HTML report (must be empty)."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Jenkins Pipeline Integration"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"To integrate JMeter into a Jenkins CI/CD pipeline, create a Jenkinsfile using Declarative Pipeline syntax. Ensure JMeter is installed on the build agent and available in the system PATH."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Checkout') {
            steps {
                git 'https://github.com/your-repo/jmeter-tests.git'
            }
        }
        stage('Run Load Test') {
            steps {
                sh 'jmeter -n -t test_plans/load_test.jmx -l build/results.jtl -e -o build/html_report'
            }
        }
    }
    post {
        always {
            publishHTML(target: [
                allowMissing: false,
                alwaysLinkToLastBuild: true,
                keepAll: true,
                reportDir: 'build/html_report',
                reportFiles: 'index.html',
                reportName: 'JMeter HTML Report'
            ])
        }
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"GitLab CI/CD Integration"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"For GitLab CI/CD, use a Docker image that has Java and JMeter pre-installed, or build a custom runner. Configure the .gitlab-ci.yml file to execute the test and save the HTML report as an artifact."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"stages:
  - performance

variables:
  JMETER_VERSION: "5.5"

run_load_test:
  stage: performance
  image: justb4/jmeter:${JMETER_VERSION}
  script:
    - jmeter -n -t test_plans/load_test.jmx -l results.jtl -e -o html_report
  artifacts:
    expire_in: 30 days
    paths:
      - html_report/
      - results.jtl"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for CI/CD Performance Testing"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always parameterize threads, ramp-up periods, and durations using JMeter properties (-J property syntax) so they can be overridden via the CLI."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Disable unnecessary listeners like View Results Tree or Graph Results in your .jmx scripts to conserve memory during headless execution."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Set up failure criteria in your pipeline using plugins or scripts that analyze the generated .jtl files for error rates and response time thresholds."}]
